<article class="detail-page"><a class="detail-back" href="/insights/">← Back to insights</a><div class="section-label">Cost & Margins</div><h1>The 5 Places Every MSME Overpays on Procurement — and How to Fix Each One</h1><div class="detail-content modal-body"><p>In every cost audit we conduct, we find the same five structural overpayments in MSME procurement. Together, they typically represent 15–30% of total procurement spend.</p><h4>1. Too Many Vendors, No Leverage</h4><p>The average MSME we work with has 120–200 active vendors. With spend fragmented across this many suppliers, you have zero negotiating power with any of them. Rationalising to 40–60 strategic vendors and consolidating spend typically delivers 8–15% price reduction within 90 days.</p><h4>2. No Formal Contracts or SLAs</h4><p>Without contracts, every repeat purchase is a new negotiation — and vendors know it. Formalising contracts with your top 20 vendors (which represent 80% of spend) locks in pricing and creates accountability for quality and delivery.</p><h4>3. Reactive vs Planned Buying</h4><p>When procurement is driven by immediate need rather than planned demand, you pay premium prices, accept poor terms, and make poor quality decisions under time pressure. An 8-week rolling procurement plan, aligned to your demand forecast, eliminates this entirely.</p><h4>4. No Benchmark Against Market</h4><p>Most MSMEs do not know whether they are paying market rates. They know only what they paid last time. Regular benchmarking — even quarterly — creates the baseline to identify overpriced categories and renegotiate with data.</p><h4>5. Payment Term Arbitrage Left on the Table</h4><p>Extending payment terms from 30 to 60 days across your vendor base frees significant working capital. Early payment discounts offered to strategic vendors in exchange for price reductions is another lever most MSMEs never use. Both are pure margin improvement with zero capital investment.</p></div><a class="btn-primary" href="/#contact">Request a Discovery Call →</a></article>
</Layout>
